<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* Without an explicit icon, browsers probe /favicon.ico on every page load.
       That 404s, and the 404 handler re-executes the whole app — returning a 4.6 KB
       HTML page in answer to a request for an icon. *@
    <link rel="icon" href="@Assets["favicon.svg"]" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <ResourcePreloader />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["AniQueue.Web.styles.css"]" />
    <ImportMap />
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveServer" />
</head>

<body>
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveServer" />
    <ReconnectModal />

    @* Deferred deliberately, and this is load-bearing rather than a micro-optimisation.
       ASP.NET writes the prerendered component state as a comment *after* </html> —
       past this tag in the byte stream. Without defer the browser may execute Blazor
       the moment it parses this line, while the tail of the document is still
       arriving, so the circuit starts with no persisted state. Every component then
       re-runs its initialiser and renders a loading state, replacing the prerendered
       markup with a structurally different tree at the exact moment the client is
       binding to it — which fails the attach with "insertBefore … parentNode is null"
       and kills the circuit. Firefox loses this race far more readily than Chromium,
       and anything that slows the response — a debugger, a cold start — widens it.

       defer moves execution to after parsing completes, so the state comment is
       always in the document before Blazor looks for it. *@
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]" defer></script>
</body>

</html>
